ounces:\n[3169] \"Mr. Cade.\" [3170] Anthony strides towards the host. [3171] \"Lord Carterham.\" He says, \"I do apologize for the inconvenience, but I believe that tonight, all secrets will be revealed.\" [3172] Lord Carterham seems relieved, and he has always had an inexplicable fondness for Anthony. [3173] \"No trouble at all.\" He says sincerely. [3174] \"You are too kind.\" Anthony says, \"Everyone is here, so I will begin.\" [3175] \"I don't understand,\" George Romax pretends to be confused, \"I don't understand. This is irregular. What is Mr. Kidd's status? The situation is delicate and complicated. I strongly suggest\u2026\"\n[3176] George's eloquent speech was interrupted by the Sheriff quietly walking over to him and whispering something. After a moment, George suddenly looked puzzled. [3177] \"Well, if you say so.\" He said reluctantly. Then, he loudly added, \"Let's hear what he has to say.\" [3178] Anthony did not let the obvious contempt bother him. [3179] \"It's just a small idea, that's all.\" He said cheerfully. \"As you all know, we received a cipher a few days ago that mentioned Richmond and some numbers. We attempted to decrypt it, but failed. Now, using the late Count Stapleton's memoirs\u2014which I happened to read\u2014we can solve this puzzle. The memoirs mentioned a dinner party with a 'flower' theme, where all the guests wore floral symbols. The Count's own badge, which we found in the strange emblem on the wall of the secret passage, represents a rose. You should remember the rows of things: buttons, the letter E, and weaving. Now, what in this villa has rows? Books, right? And in the Earl of Carrington's library catalog, there is a book titled 'The Earl of Richmond.' Now you all know where the hidden jewels are. From that book, using those numbers to search the bookshelves and book numbers, our long-sought item should be hidden in a fake book or a cave behind a book.\" [3180] Anthony modestly looked around, clearly waiting for applause