nds last time.\" [1124] \"What case was that?\" The Earl of Carrington asked with interest. [1125] But George's gaze had already turned to Bunder, who was sitting at the window, half inside and half outside. He suddenly realized that caution was needed and stood up. [1126] \"Don't waste time. I'll send a telegram right away.\" [1127] \"You come down and let Bandel send it.\" [1128] George took out a pen and quickly wrote a few lines, then handed the first telegram to Bandel, who looked curiously at it. [1129] \"Oh, what's this name? What's this baron?\" she asked. [1130] \"Lord Lolopryte Jeechill.\" [1131] Bandler blinked. [1132] \"I see, it still needs to be sent to the post office.\" [1133] George didn't stop writing and handed the newly written content to Bandler, then addressed the villa's owner:\n[1134] \"Carterham, the best thing you can do is\u2026\"\n[1135] \"Speak,\" the Earl of Carterham said anxiously. [1136] \"Just give everything to me.\" [1137] \"No problem,\" the Earl of Carterham replied promptly, \"I was thinking the same thing. The police and Dr. Cartwright are in the council chamber, and\u2026 uh\u2026 the corpse. Dear Romax, I am entrusting the chimney villa to you in full, everything is up to you.\" [1138] \"Thank you,\" George said, \"if I need to consult you on anything\u2026\"\n[1139] Before he could finish, the Earl of Carterham had silently slipped out and disappeared, and Bandel slyly watched her father retreat. [1140] \"I'll go send a telegram,\" she said. \"Do you know the way to the council chamber?\" [1141] \"Thank you, Miss Elaine,\" said George. [1142] George hurried out of the room. [1143] * * *\n[1144] [1] Refers to George Romax. [1145] [2] Battle can be both a name, \"Battles,\" and a word meaning \"fight\" in English. [1146] Chapter 11: The Appearance of Sheriff Battler\n[1147] The Earl of Carringham spent the entire morning wandering around his estate to avoid George, until he felt hungry and returned. He thought the worst should have been over by now. [1148] He sneaked into the house through a side door and then tiptoed into his own secret room. He was proud of his stealthy actions, but he was mistaken. Tredwell was a man who watched everything, and he immediately appeared at the Earl's door. [1149] \"Excuse me, sir.\" [1150] \"What is it, Tredwell?\" [1151]\u201cRomeyax is eager to see you and asks that you return to the study to meet him.\u201d\n[1152]Tredwell subtly conveyed that if the Earl had no objection, he could inform Romeyax that the Earl of Carrington had not yet returned. [1153]The Earl of Carrington sighed and stood up. [1154]\u201cI suppose it's inevitable. You said the study, right?\u201d\n[1155]\u201cYes, sir.\u201d\n[1156] The Earl of Carringham sighed again and walked towards the study in his spacious ancestral home. When he arrived at the study, he found that the door was locked. He turned the door handle, and then someone inside opened the door from the inside. The door opened a crack, and George Roxburgh's face appeared. He peered out suspiciously. [1157] It was only when he recognized Carringham that his expression changed. [1158] \"Ah, Carringham, come in. We were just wondering how you were.\" [1159] As Carringham entered the room, he slightly apologized and vaguely said that he had gone to the manor to do some repairs for the tenants. In addition to George, there were two other people in the room. One was Major McRoss, the local police chief; the other was a middle-aged man with a muscular build, a stern expression, but an air of nobility. [1160] \"Bartle came half an hour ago,\" George said. \"He and Inspector Baggott have been around the scene and have seen Dr. Carterwright. He now wants to ask us a few questions.\" [1161] Carringham Earl greeted Macross and introduced himself to Inspector Barter. After that, everyone sat down. [1162] \"Inspector Barter, I don't need to say much,\" George said. \"This must be kept absolutely confidential.\" [1163] The inspector