eferential knock. He stood at the door, looking stern, and addressed his master:\n[1227] \"Excuse me, sir. A gentleman wishes to see you, with urgent matters related to this morning's tragedy.\" [1228] \"What's his name?\" Sheriff Battert suddenly asked. [1229] \"His name is Anthony Kade, but he said it doesn't hold any particular meaning to anyone.\" [1230] However, the name seemed to hold a special meaning for the four people present. They all stood up, surprised. [1231] The Earl of Cartonham chuckled. [1232] \"I find this increasingly amusing, Traddles. Please show Mr. Kedge in immediately.\" [1233] Chapter Twelve: Anthony's Story\n[1234] \"Mr. Anthony Kedge,\" Traddles announced. [1235] \"A suspicious stranger from a country inn,\" Anthony said. [1236] With an unusual intuition about strangers, he walked directly towards the Earl of Carringham. At the same time, he mentally summarized the other three people: one was from the Metropolitan Police, likely the police commissioner; another was a local dignitary, and the last one seemed to be on the verge of madness and was probably related to the government. [1237] \"I must apologize for barging in like this,\" Anthony continued, addressing the Earl of Carringham. \"There have been rumors of a murder here, or at 'The Happy Hound,' as it's called, the local inn. I thought I might have some relevant information, so I came to investigate.\" [1238] There was a moment of silence for a minute or two. Constable Batter didn't speak because he was experienced enough to let others speak first to gain the upper hand; Colonel MacRoss didn't speak because he was naturally taciturn; George didn't speak because he was used to listening to others' reports; and the Earl of Carringham didn't speak because he truly didn't know what to say. However, the silence of the other three put him on the spot, and since the other person was addressing him directly, the Earl of Carringham finally had to speak up:\n[1239] \"Ah, yes, yes,\" he said 