t.\" [1262] \"Yes, Mr. Kidd,\" the Earl of Carringham asked curiously, \"what were you doing?\" [1263] \"Well,\" Anthony said regretfully, \"that's a long story.\" [1264] He took out his cigarette case. [1265] \"May I smoke?\" [1266] The Marquess of Carringham nodded, and Anthony lit a cigarette, preparing for the upcoming test. [1267] He was well aware of his precarious situation. In the span of just twenty-four hours, he had been involved in two different murders. The first one he could not escape, having intentionally left the body behind and evading law enforcement before arriving at the scene of the second crime. For a young man looking for trouble, it was a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity. [1268] \"South America,\u201d Anthony thought to himself, \"has nothing to do with this.\" [1269] He had decided on his tactic. He would tell the truth, but with a slight modification and a major omission. [1270] \"It all started about three weeks ago,\" Anthony said, \"in Bulawayo.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHis appearance was all a facade. Lord Carterham mentioned that although he came here to see the first edition books, he never spoke when the topic was brought up. There are several suspicious things. There was light in his window on the night of the murder. And on the night of the incident in the conference hall, when I met him on the balcony, he was dressed up. The note could have been dropped by him, and you didn't see it was Mr. Kade who dropped it. Mr. Kade might have gone to Dover to investigate, and he might have been kidnapped there. I think Mr. Fuchs is much more suspicious than Mr. Kade.\" [2974] The Frenchman said sternly:\n[2975] \"From your point of view, maybe so, but I won't argue with you. I also agree that Mr. Fuchs is not what he seems to be.\" [2976] \"So, what then?\" [2977] \"