howed a slight surprise. [3075] \"Oh!\" he said uncertainly. \"I'll go and see.\" [3076] \"Tell him it's urgent,\" Anthony said. [3077] \"Do you have a letter of recommendation from the Earl of Carrington?\" the young man asked. [3078] \"I do have something like that,\" Anthony said. \"But I need to see Mr. Issakov immediately.\" [3079] Two minutes later, Anthony was led into a luxurious inner room, which was impressively furnished with large leather armchairs. [3080] Mr. Issakov rose to greet him. [3081] \"I hope you'll forgive this impromptu visit,\" Anthony said. \"I know you're busy, so I won't take up too much of your valuable time. I just have a business matter to discuss with you.\" [3082] Mr. Issakovitch fixed him with his bright black eyes. [3083] \"Have a smoke,\" he said abruptly, pulling out an open cigar box. [3084] \"Thank you,\" Anthony said. \"Please give me one.\" [3085] He took one out himself. [3086] \"This deal is related to Hesslegrave.\" Anthony said as he took the match, noticing Azerbaijan's determined eyes brightening up. [3087] \"The murder of Michael Prince must have ruined the whole plan.\" [3088] Mr. Azerbaijan raised his eyebrows and whispered, \"What?\" with a questioning look towards the ceiling. [3089] \"Oil.\" Anthony thoughtfully looked at the smooth table, \"Oil, what an amazing thing!\" [3090] He felt the financier slightly startled. [3091] \"Well, let's get straight to the point, shall we?\" [3092] \"Of course, Eisakstan, if those oil rights go to another company, I'm sure you wouldn't be pleased?\" [3093] \"What do you suggest?\" The other man looked him straight in the eye. [3094] \"Find a suitable heir to the throne, someone who is pro-English in attitude.\" [3095] \"Where do you suggest we find one?\" [3096] \"That's my business.\" [3097] Mr. Issaquena smiled slightly in agreement, his eyes becoming more stern and piercing. [3098] \"Is it genuine? I won't tolerate any jokes about this, do you understand?\" [3099] \"Absolutely genuine.\" [3100] \"Cash deal?\" [3101] \"Cash deal.\" [3102] \"Done deal.\" [3103] \"I don't seem to need to say much to convince you?\" Anthony curiously looked at him. [3104] Herman Isaacstan smiled. [3105] \"If I couldn't tell the truth from a lie, I wouldn't be in the position I am today.\" He said briefly, \"What are your terms?\" [3106] \"The same loan, the same terms, as you offered to Prince Michael.\" [3107] \"And yourself?\" [3108] \"For now, nothing. Just that you come to the Chimney Villa tonight.\" [3109] \"No,\" Essenstatten refused firmly, \"This is a definite no.\" [3110] \"Why?\" [3111] \"I have an important dinner engagement.\" [3112] \"Still, it would be best if you canceled that dinner. It would be better for you.\" [3113] \"What do you mean?\" [3114] Anthony looked at him for a full minute, then slowly said:\n[3115] \"Do you know that the gun has been found? The gun that killed Prince Michael. Do you know where it was found? In your suitcase.\" [3116] \"What?\" Isakov nearly jumped out of his chair, his face contorted with anger as he asked:\n[3117] \"What are you talking about? What does that mean?\" [3118] \"Let me explain.\" [3119] Anthony recounted the discovery of the gun to Isakov, who turned pale and looked terrified. [3120] \"It's all a lie.\" Isakov exclaimed after Anthony finished speaking. [3121] \"Don't get upset,\" Anthony soothed him, \"if that's the case, it's easy for you to prove your innocence.\" [3122] \"How?\" asked Isakov, perplexed. [3123] \"If I were you,\" said Anthony calmly, \"I'd go to the chimney house tonight.\" [3124] Isakov hesitated, looking at him uncertainly. [3125]\n[3126] \"You suggest I do that?\" [3127] Anthony leaned in close to Isaacstan's ear and whispered a few words. The financial expert was stunned, staring at him in disbelief. [3128] \"Are you really going to\u2026\"\n[3129] \"You'll see,\" Anthony said. [3130] Chapter Twenty-seven, October Thirteen (Continued)\n[3131] The clock in the council chamber struck nine. [3132] \"Ah,\" the Earl of Carrington sighed deeply, \"they're back, like sheep, wagging their tails one after the other, all back.\" [3133] He looked sadly around the room. [3134] \"A street singer with a monkey,\" he muttered, staring at the baron, \"and a busybody from Shiregrove Street\u2026\"\n[3135] \"That's not very friendly to say about the baron,\" Bunder protested after hearing his complaints. \"He told me you were the perfect embodiment of the British aristocracy's hospitality.\" [3136] \"I think,\u201d said the Earl of Chatham, \"he says that to everyone, without getting tired. But I'm no longer the hospitable British gentleman I used to be. Once I sell the chimney house to an American entrepreneur, I'll move to a hotel. There, if someone bothers you, you can settle the bill and leave right away.\" [3137] \"Cheer up,\u201d said Bunder, \"we may never see Mr. Fischer again.\" [3138] \"I always found him an interesting person,\u201d said the Earl of Chatham, his mood fluctuating, \"it's all your fault, you young people, for making me get involved in these things. Why did they have to hold their board meeting in my house? Why couldn't they go to Rousham, Hume Castle, or find a good villa in Stretton to hold their meeting?\" [3139] \"The atmosphere isn't right there,\u201d said Bunder.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere aren't many British people who can climb trees.\" [985] He then returned to London and Paddington Station, storing the suitcase in another cloakroom at the arrival hall. He was now eager to have a delicious sirloin steak, juicy meat, and a large portion of fries. However, he looked at his watch and sadly shook his head. He filled the Morris with gas and set off again. This time, he headed north. [986] He parked on the side of the road near the garden of the Chimney House at exactly 11:30. He jumped out of the car and effortlessly scaled the wall, running towards the house. He had taken longer than he had anticipated, so he started to run. In the darkness, a looming gray structure could be seen in the distance, which was the imposing architecture of the Chimney House. The chimes of a clock could be heard in the distance, indicating that another fifteen minutes had passed. [987] At 11:45, it was the time mentioned in the note. Anthony had reached the platform in front of the house. He looked up at the darkened building and everything was silent. [988] \"These politicians go to bed so early,\" he muttered to himself. [989] Suddenly, a sound entered his ears, a gunshot. [990] Anthony quickly looked around, the sound had come from inside the house. He waited for a moment, but it was still deathly quiet. [991] He walked up to a ground-floor window, judging that the gunshot came from this room. He tried the handle, but the window was locked, so he tried other windows. He remained focused on the surrounding noises, but it was still as quiet as ever. [992] Finally, he began to doubt that the gunshot was imagined by himself or perhaps the sound of someone hunting in the forest. He turned back to the courtyard, feeling a vague sense of dissatisfaction and unease. [993] He looked back at the house, and suddenly a light came on in one of the second-floor rooms, but it went out in an instant. The place was back to darkness. [994] Chapter 10: The Chimney House\n[995] It was 8:30 AM, and Inspector Bagshaw was already in his office. He was tall and muscular, always presenting a matter-of-fact demeanor. Whenever a case was tense, his breathing would become heavier. Johnson was his constable, a new recruit, and had a somewhat unconfident and unassured look, like a weak chick. [996] The phone on the desk suddenly rang, and Inspector Bajwa answered it with his usual gruffness. [997] \"Yes, this is the Baystar Market Police Station, I am Inspector Bajwa. What's the matter?\" [998] His tone shifted subtly. As a senior officer, he had authority over Johnson, and someone else had authority over him. [999] \"Please, Your Lordship, could you repeat that? I didn't quite catch it.\" [1000] There was a long pause as the inspector listened intently, his usually expressionless face shifting through various complex emotions. Finally, he succinctly said, \"I'll take care of it right away, Your Lordship,\" and hung up the phone. [1001] He turned to Johnson, looking serious. [1002] \"It was the Earl of Chimney House who called. A murder case.\" [1003] Johnson displayed appropriate shock, repeating, \"A murder case.\" [1004] \"That's right, a murder case,\" the inspector confirmed. [1005] \"But this area has never had a murder case before, at least not that I'm aware of. Oh, except for that time Tom Pearce shot his lover.\" [1006] \"Strictly speaking, that wasn't a murder case. That was an accident caused by excessive drinking.\" The inspector didn't agree. [1007] \"He wasn't hanged for it.\" Johnson agreed, disappointedly, \"But this time it's for real, isn't it?\" [1008] \"Yes, it's for real. The victim was a guest of the Earl, a foreigner. The window was open, and there were footprints outside.\" [1009] \"It's a pity it's a foreigner.\" Johnson was a bit disappointed. [1010] With this, the murder case seemed less real to Johnson, as foreigners were easily shot in his opinion. [1011] \"The Earl has never been so anxious,\" the inspector continued. \"Call Dr. Carter-Laithe, and we'll leave immediately. Hopefully, the footprints will still be intact.\" [1012] Bagginswood was almost giddy with excitement. A murder! The Manor House! Bagginswood Inspector in charge! The police found a lead, and then there was a city-wide arrest. Advancement and honors would follow. [1013] \"It's perfect,\" Bagginswood Inspector thought to himself. \"As long as the Metropolitan Police don't interfere.\" [1014] This thought brought him down. It was highly likely to happen in this situation. [1015] They found Dr. Carter-Laithe, and the young man showed a strong interest, his reaction matching Johnson's. [1016] \"Oh, my goodness!\" he exclaimed. \"There hasn't been a murder case since the Tom Pearce incident.\" [1017] The three of them squeezed into the doctor's car and set off for the Chimney House. As they passed the local inn called \"The Happy Cricketer,\" the doctor noticed a stranger standing at the door. [1018] \"A stranger,\" he said. \"Looks good, I don't know how long he's been here, what's he doing at 'The Cricketer's' door? I've never seen him before, he must have arrived last night.\" [1019] \"He didn't come by train,\" Johnson said. [1020] His brother was the local station porter, so Johnson knew everyone who came and went. [1021] \"Who came here yesterday to go to the Chimney House?\" the inspector asked. [1022] \"Lady Elinor, she took the 3:40 train, with two gentlemen, one American, one young soldier. They both didn't have male servants. The Earl and a foreign gentleman went together, along with the foreigner's servant. They took the 5:40 train. This foreigner might be the victim. Mr. Everleigh also took that train. Mrs. Ruff took the 7:25 train, and another bald, hooked-nose, foreign-looking gentleman was on that one too. Mrs. Ruff's maid took the 8:56 train.\" [1023] Johnson stopped and took a breath. [1024] \"Is anyone staying at 'The Happy Cricketer'?\" [1025] Johnson shook his head. [1026] \"So he must have come by car,\" said the inspector. \"Johnson, make a note, when you return, go to 'The Happy Wanderer' and investigate. We need to know about all the strangers. That man with the dark skin might be a foreigner too.\" [1027] The inspector nodded sagely, as if to suggest that he was of the type who thought things through perfectly and was extremely astute. [1028] The car passed through the gates and entered the courtyard of the Chimney House. This historic site, described in detail in any travel guide, is third in the list of British historic mansions and costs 21 shillings for admission. Every Thursday, visitors from Midlingham take the long-distance bus to visit the open parts of the house. For this reason, there is no need to elaborate on the Chimney House. [1029] A silver-haired butler greeted them at the door, his movements flawless, as if to say, \"It's not usual for something like this to happen here, but we must remain calm and composed in the face of disaster.\" [1030] \"The Earl is waiting for you,\" said the butler. \"This way, please.\" [1031] They were led to a comfortable little room where Sir Carterham was waiting to escape the \"storm outside.\" [1032] \"Sir, the police are here, and Dr. Carterright,\" said the butler. [1033] Sir Carterham was pacing nervously around the room. [1034] \"Ah, Inspector, you've finally arrived, thank God. How are you, Dr. Carterright? This is so terrible, so terrible.\" [1035] Sir Carterham was frantically pulling at his hair, making it stand on end. He looked even less like a nobleman at this point. [1036] \"Where is the body?\" the doctor asked briskly, in a professional manner. [1037] This straightforward question seemed to relax the Earl of Carringham, and he turned to face Dr. Carruthers. [1038] \"In the grand hall, the body was found there, and I had everyone not disturb the scene. I thought it best.\" [1039] \"You did very well, my lord,\" the inspector commended. [1040] He took out his notebook and pencil. [1041] \"Who found the body? Was it you?\" [1042] \"Thank goodness, no,\" the Earl of Carrington said. \"I usually don't get up so early. It was a maid who found the body. She made a lot of noise, but I didn't hear it. They came to get me, and then I got up and came downstairs. That's the story.\" [1043] \"Was he a guest of yours? Can you identify him?\" [1044] \"Yes, Inspector,\" the Earl said. [1045] \"What is his name?\" [1046] This simple question seemed to unsettle Lord Carmarthen, who opened and closed his mouth several times before finally asking, \"Do you mean to ask me, what is his name?\" [1047] \"Yes, my lord.\" [1048] \"Well, this... Lord Carmarthen took a slow look around the room, as if gathering his thoughts. \"His name is, I should say, yes, it's... Stanislaus Count.\" [1049] Lord Carmarthen's reaction was too unusual, and the inspector paused with his pen, staring intently at him. At that moment, the doorbell rang, breaking the awkwardness. [1050] A young girl entered the room, tall and thin, with brown skin and a pretty face. She was Lady Elinor Brent, also known as Bendl, Lord Carmarthen's eldest daughter. She nodded to the others and spoke directly to her father, \"I found him.\" [1051] For a moment, the inspector thought the girl had caught the killer in the act and almost rushed forward, but he quickly realized that was not what she meant. [1052] Carrington, the Earl of Carrington, breathed a sigh of relief. [1053] \"Good job. What did he say?\" [1054] \"He's on his way. He told us to be very careful.\" [1055] His father sighed in frustration. [1056] \"George Romax would say such stupid things. But once he arrives, I can leave it to him.\" [1057] This thought seemed to cheer him up a bit. [1058] \"Is the victim's name Count Stanislaus, right?\" the doctor asked. [1059] Father and daughter exchanged a quick glance, and then the father answered solemnly:\n[1060] \"Yes, I just said that.\" [1061] \"I asked again because you seemed unsure just now,\" Carteret explained. [1062] The Earl of Carrington's eyes flashed with a reproachful look as he looked at him. [1063] \"I'll take you to the banquet hall,\" he said, his tone lightening. [1064] They followed him to the banquet hall, with the inspector trailing behind, keeping a watchful eye on every corner that might hold a clue, from the frames to the doors. [1065] The Earl of Carrington took a key from his pocket, unlocked the door, and pushed it open, revealing a room with walls paneled in oak and a terrace with three large windows. In the room, there was a long dining table, some oak cabinets, and several elegant antique chairs. The walls were adorned with portraits of deceased members of the Carrington family and other notable individuals. [1066] On the floor, in the middle of the room, a man lay on his back with his arms spread out. [1067] Dr. Carterwright approached the body and knelt down to examine it closely. The inspector walked over to the windows and inspected each one in turn. The middle window was closed but not latched, and there were footprints leading up to the window and away from it on the outside steps. [1068] \"It's clear,\" the inspector nodded, \"but there should be footprints inside as well. The hardwood floor would be a perfect surface for them.\" [1069] \"As for that,\" Bunder interjected, \"the maid only discovered the body after she had cleaned half the floor this morning. She came in when it was still dark and went straight to the window to pull back the curtains and start cleaning. The body was on the other side of the room, behind the table, and she couldn't see it until she stood up and looked over the table.\" [1070] The inspector nodded. [1071] \"Alright,\" Sir Carterham is eager to leave, \"Inspector, you take care of this. Call me if you need me. But George Romax will be here from Weefen Abbey soon. He knows more about it than I do. It's his business, after all. I can't explain it as well as he can.\" [1072] Before the other person can respond, Sir Carterham hurries away. [1073] \"Romax is a nuisance,\" he complains. \"Dragging me into this. Anything, Mr. Tredwell?\" [1074] The white-haired butler follows him closely. [1075] \"Sir, I took the liberty of moving breakfast up earlier for you, and everything is ready in the dining room.\" [1076] \"I can't eat,\" the Earl of Carringham said glumly as he walked towards the dining room. \"I can't eat a thing.\" [1077] Bunder wrapped his arm around his, and they walked into the dining room. Several heavy silver platters of warming food were laid out on the sideboard. [1078] \"Omelets,\" the Earl of Carringham lifted the lids one by one. \"Eggs and bacon, kidneys, spicy chicken, black cod, cold ham, cold pheasant. Nothing I like. Tredwell, can you ask the chef to make me a poached egg, please?\" [1079] \"Of course, sir.\" [1080] Tredwell withdrew. The Earl of Carringham absent-mindedly loaded up on kidneys and ham, poured himself a cup of coffee, and sat down at the table. Bunder filled a plate with eggs and bacon. [1081] \"I'm starving,\" said Bondell, with his mouth full, \"probably too excited.\" [1082] \"You seem to be enjoying yourself,\" his father complained, \"you young people always like excitement. My nerves can't stand it. Dr. Abner Willis told me not to worry. He just sits in his Harley Street consulting room and talks nonsense. How can I not worry? Mr. Roxemax got me into this mess, and I should have stuck to my guns.\" [1083] The Marquess of Carringham shook his head in frustration, stood up, and cut himself another dish of ham. [1084] \"Cordes[1] is making a fool of himself,\" Bondell said excitedly, \"he was already talking nonsense on the phone. When he gets here in a couple of minutes, he'll go on and on about being careful and not making a fuss.\" [1085] The Marquess of Carringham grunted at the thought. [1086] \"Did he get up?\" he asks. [1087] \"He said,\" Bunder answers, \"He got up early, dictating letters and memos since seven o'clock.\" [1088] \"That's impressive,\" her father says, \"These politicians are so selfish. Their secretaries get up early every day to read them a bunch of useless crap. If they could pass a law making them not get up before eleven, that would be a boon to the nation. If they could stop talking nonsense, I'd be fine. Roxemus keeps talking about my status, as if I really have any standing. Who wants to be a noble these days?\" [1089] \"Nobody,\" Bunder says, \"I'd rather run a thriving pub.\" [1090] Tredwell quietly approaches, carrying a small silver dish with two poached eggs, and places it in front of the Earl of Carringham. [1091] \"What is this, Trudwell?\" the Earl of Cartonham asked with a slight look of disgust at the poached eggs. [1092] \"Poached eggs, sir.\" [1093] \"I hate poached eggs,\" the Earl of Cartonham said angrily. \"They have no flavor. I don't even want to look at them. Take them away, Trudwell.\" [1094] \"Yes, sir.\" [1095] Trudwell took the poached eggs and quietly withdrew. [1096] \"It's a good thing no one else in this house gets up that early,\" the Earl of Carrington said sincerely. \"I'll have to tell them the bad news when they do. He sighed. [1097] \"I wonder who killed him,\" Bunder said. [1098] \"At least, it's none of our business,\" the Earl of Carrington said. \"Let the police handle it. I don't think Inspector Bajewski will find anything. I suspect it was Esarhaddon.\" [1099] \"You mean?\" [1100] \"The representative of the Anglo-American Bank Group.\" [1101] \"How could Essex kill him?\" asked the Marquess. \"He came here to see him. That's too complicated,\" he said vaguely. \"I'm not surprised that Essex is late. He could appear at any moment. That's a habit of city people. No matter how rich a person is, they have to work from nine to five.\" [1102] The sound of a speeding car's engine came through the open window. [1103] \"Kodis,\" shouted Bunder. [1104] The car stopped at the gate, and the father and daughter leaned out of the window to wave to the person inside. [1105] \"Over here, old friend,\" called the Marquess of Carringham, quickly swallowing his ham. [1106] Mr. Kodges didn't intend to crawl through the window, so he walked through the front door. Not long after, Trudwell brought him into the room and then quickly left. [1107] \"Have some breakfast,\" the Marquess of Carrington said, shaking his hand. \"How about some kidneys?\" [1108] Mr. Kodges had no patience for kidneys. [1109] \"It's a disaster, terrible, terrible,\" he said. [1110] \"Indeed,\" the Marquess of Carrington replied. \"Care for some cod?\" [1111] \"No, don't. This must be kept secret, no matter what.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nMr. Romax is likely familiar with the place, it's the British outpost, a 'only the English know England' kind of place, where I was chatting with a friend named Jimmy McGregor.\" He enunciated the name carefully, casting a meaningful glance at George. George was startled but managed to suppress a gasp. [1271] \"The conclusion of our conversation was that he couldn't come himself, so I came to England to do a small favor for Mr. McGregor. Since the ship tickets were booked under his name, I traveled under the name Jimmy McGregor. I don't know if that's illegal, Inspector, but you can tell me if it is. If necessary, they might put me in jail for a few months.\" [1272] \"Shall we continue, sir?\" Inspector Batter asked, but his eyes flickered slightly. [1273] \"After arriving in London, I checked into the Britz Hotel, still using James McGregor's name. My task in London was to deliver a manuscript to a publisher. But almost as soon as I arrived, representatives from two foreign parties found me. One was reasonable and legal, while the other was not. I dealt with them accordingly. But my troubles were not over. That night, a hotel employee broke into my room to steal my things.\" [1274] \"You didn't report it, did you?\" Inspector Batter asked. [1275] \"No, I didn't. Nothing was stolen. But I told the hotel manager about it, and he can vouch for me, and tell you how the employee suddenly disappeared in the middle of the night. The next day, the publisher called me, suggesting they send someone to pick up the manuscript from me, which I agreed to. So the next morning, we proceeded accordingly. Since I haven't received any further information, I assume they have received the manuscript safely. Yesterday, I received a letter from Mr. Roxburgh, still under the name James McGregor\u2026\"\n[1276] Anthony paused. He was beginning to find the situation interesting. George fidgeted uncomfortably in his seat. [1277] \"I have a vague recollection,\" he murmured. \"There were many letters, although the name was different. I might not have remembered it, but I must say, I find impersonating another person to be highly improper. I believe, without a doubt, you have committed a serious crime.\" [1278] \"In this letter,\" Anthony continued unfazed, \"Mr. Roxby made several suggestions about the manuscript in my possession and invited me to attend a banquet at the Earl of Carrington's residence.\" [1279] \"It's a pleasure to meet you,\" said the Earl. \"Better late than never, right?\" [1280] George frowned at him. [1281] Inspector Batterton continues to stare at Anthony. [1282] \"Is that your explanation for being here last night?\" he asks. [1283] \"Of course not,\" Anthony replies mildly. \"I was invited to the villa, and I wouldn't climb walls, trample lawns, and look through windows in the middle of the night; I would drive to the door, ring the bell, wipe my shoes on the doormat, and enter with dignity. I'll continue, I wrote back to Mr. Roxemus, explaining that the manuscript was no longer in my possession and that I regretfully declined the Earl of Chatham's kind invitation. But then, I remembered something.\" [1284] Inspector Batterton carefully examines the piece of paper. [1285] \"Of course,\" Anthony continues, \"Chimney House may have nothing to do with this house, but it may also have something to do with it. And Gisippus is a thief and a scoundrel. So, last night, I decided to come here to see if everything was in order, stay at the inn overnight, and then visit the Earl of Chatham in the morning to ask him to strengthen his defenses so that no one comes to disturb him on the weekend.\" [1286] \"Quite right,\" the Duke of Carringham encouraged. [1287] \"I arrived late, and there was not enough time. So I parked and climbed over the wall and through the lawn. When I reached the platform, the whole house was pitch black, and there was no sound. As soon as I turned around, I heard a gunshot. I thought it came from inside the house, so I ran back, crossed the platform, and tried the windows. But the windows were locked, and there was no movement inside. I waited for a while, and the whole house was as quiet as a grave, so I thought I might have misheard it, perhaps the sound of a poacher's gun. In such a situation, such a thought is quite normal.\" [1288] \"Quite normal,\" Chief Balthasar said without expression. [1289] \"I went back to the inn and spent the night there. This morning, I heard the news. Of course, I know I am a suspect, and that is inevitable in such a situation, so I came over and told my story, hoping not to be a scapegoat and be handcuffed.\" [1290] There was a moment of silence, and Colonel Mallowe turned his head to look at Chief Balthasar. [1291] \"I think this story is clear,\" he said. [1292] \"Yes,\" said the sheriff, \"I think we won't be cuffing anyone this morning.\" [1293] \"Is there anything else, Sheriff Bartholomew?\" [1294] \"I just want to know one thing. What was that document?\" [1295] He looked at George, who reluctantly answered, \"It was a letter.\" [1296] \"It's the memoirs of the late Count Stapleton. You know... \"\n[1297] \"No need to elaborate,\" Balthasar said, \"I understand.\" [1298] He turned to Anthony and said, \"Do you know who was shot, Mr. Kidd?\" [1299] \"At the 'Happy Dog Pub,' they say it was someone who wasn't either Count Stanislaus or Count something else.\" [1300] \"Tell him,\" Balthasar briefly said to George Roxbury. [1301] It was clear that George was reluctant, but he had to say, \"The man here disguised as Count Stanislaus is Prince Michael of Hesse.\" [1302] Anthony whistled in surprise. [1303] \"That's awkward,\" he said. [1304] The sheriff, Bartle, who had been closely observing Anthony, grunted contentedly, as if something had pleased him. He suddenly stood up. [1305] \"I have a couple of questions for Mr. Kidd,\" he announced. \"May I take him to the council chamber?\" [1306] \"Of course, of course,\" said the Earl of Carringham. \"You may take him wherever you like.\" [1307] Anthony and Chief Constable Bartlett went out together. [1308] The body had been removed from the scene of the tragedy. The floor where the corpse had lain was stained with a dark blot of blood, but there was no other sign of what had happened. Sunlight streamed through the three windows, casting a soft glow on the old paneling. Anthony looked around appreciatively. [1309] \"Very elegant,\" he said. \"Nothing can compare to old England, can it?\" [1310] Chief Constable Bartlett did not respond to Anthony's compliment but asked, \"Did you initially think the gunshot came from this room?\" [1311] \"Let me think.\" [1312] Anthony opened the window and stepped out onto the terrace, looking up at the house. [1313] \"Yes, it was this room,\" he said. \"This room was added later, taking up the whole corner. If it wasn't this room, the gunshot would have sounded like it came from the left. But the sound came from behind me, which is also to the right. That's why I thought of the poacher. You know, this room is at the very end of the house on the side.\" [1314] He stepped back over the threshold of the French doors and back into the room. Suddenly, he asked, \"Why do you ask? You know he was shot here, don't you?\" [1315] \"Ah!\" said the Chief Inspector. \"We can't know everything we want to know. But yes, he was shot here. You said you tried the windows, didn't you?\" [1316] \"Yes, they were all locked from the inside.\" [1317] \"You tried how many?\" [1318] \"Three of them.\" [1319] \"You sure?\" [1320] \"Very sure. Why ask?\" [1321] 