] \"His story is quite honest, and there is some truth to it that we can confirm. On the surface, the reason he was here last night makes sense. Of course, I'll wire South America to investigate his background.\" [1377] \"So, you think he's not a suspect in the murder?\" [1378] Bartlett raised his large, square hand. [1379] \"Don't jump to conclusions. I didn't say that.\" [1380] \"What do you think of this murder case, Bartlett Inspector?\" This was the first time Isakstani spoke. [1381] His voice was deep and resonant, with a persuasive power that had allowed him to establish himself in the boardroom at a young age. [1382] \"Mr. Esakstan, it's too early to draw conclusions. I haven't figured out the first question yet.\" [1383] \"What question?\" [1384] \"The motive, just like in any murder case. Who stands to gain the most from Michael's death? Only by understanding that can we solve everything.\" [1385] \"The Hesslovian revolutionaries...\" George said. [1386] Sheriff Battel did not show his usual deference and ignored his words. [1387] \"It's definitely not the Red Hand Brotherhood.\" [1388] \"But what about the red-hand-printed letter?\" [1389] \"It was left there as a deliberate piece of evidence to confuse the issue.\" [1390] George's pride was hurt. \"Battel, I don't understand why you're so certain.\" [1391] \"Mr. Roxas, we know all about the Red Hand Brotherhood. We've been keeping a close eye on them since Prince Michael to Britain. That's basic police work. They wouldn't even be within a mile of the Prince.\" [1392] \"I agree with Chief Balthar's assessment.\" Esquistan said, \"We need to think differently.\" [1393] \"You know, sir. It's encouraging to have your support, but we still know so little about this. If we don't know who benefits from Prince Michael's death, we need to know who suffers from it.\" [1394] \"What do you mean?\" Esquistan said. His black eyes fixed on Balthar, making Balthar think of a cobra in a hood. [1395] \"You two haven't mentioned the Hessian royalists. Pardon me for saying so, but you've entered a mental blind spot.\" [1396] \"Indeed, Batal,\" said George, surprised. [1397] \"Go on, Batal,\" said Issacson. \"Blind spot is an apt term. You're a smart man.\" [1398] \"You need a king, and now you've lost one!\" He snapped his fingers. \"You'll be eager to find another, but that won't be easy. I don't want to know the details of your plan, just the gist. And I suppose, it's a big deal, isn't it?\" [1399] Issacson slowly nodded. [1400] \"It's a big deal.\" [1401] \"That leads me to another question, who will be the heir to the Hessian throne?\" [1402] Isak looked across at Roxanne, who hesitated before reluctantly answering the question. [1403] \"Probably... I think... very likely, it will be Prince Nikolai.\" [1404] \"Oh,\" the sheriff said. \"Who is Prince Nikolai?\" [1405] \"He's the cousin of Prince Michael.\" [1406] \"Ah!\" said Batteau. \"I'd like to hear all about Prince Nicholas, especially where he is now.\" [1407] \"We don't know much,\" said Roemex. \"He was very eccentric when he was young. He always mixed with socialists and republicans, doing very outrageous things.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[287] \"It could be even worse,\" he continued, taking a breath. \"Suppose, just suppose, that the unfortunate disappearance incident is dug up again. You know what I'm talking about, right?\" [288] The Marquess of Carrington stared at him intently. [289] \"I don't understand. What disappearance incident?\" [290] \"You must have heard about it. It happened when they were all at the chimney villa. Henry was very worried, and his career was almost ruined because of that incident.\" [291] \"This sounds so intriguing,\" said the Marquess of Carrington. \"Who or what disappeared?\" [292] Luo Maximus leaned forward, bringing his mouth close to the Marquess's ear. The Marquess stepped back hastily. \"Oh, don't whisper in my ear like that.\" [293] \"Did you hear that?\" [294] \"Yes, I heard it,\" the Marquess of Carrington said with disgust. \"I remember hearing some strange rumors at the time. I've always wondered who could have done it, and that thing hasn't been found yet?\" [295] \"No, it hasn't. We'll handle this matter with utmost caution and won't let any wind of it get out. The Earl of Stapleton was also present, and although he didn't know much about it, he did know a little. If he held a grudge and made that incident public, think about the scandal it would cause and the far-reaching impact it would have. Everyone would ask why we're keeping it under wraps.\" [296] \"Of course they would,\" said the Marquess of Carringham with enthusiasm. [297] Lord Roxburgh's voice rose a few octaves as he struggled to control himself. [298] \"I must keep my cool,\" he muttered. \"I must keep my cool. What if he had no ill intentions and went to all this trouble to send the manuscript to London?\" [299] \"That is quite strange. Are you sure all of this is true?\" [300] \"Absolutely true. We have agents in Paris. The memoir was secretly moved a few weeks before his death.\" [301] \"It does seem there is more to it,\" said the Marquess of Carrington, regaining some of his earlier enthusiasm. [302] \"We found that the manuscripts were sent to a man named Jimmy, or James, a Canadian now living in Africa.\" [303] \"This is quite elaborate,\" said the Marquess of Carrington, clearly pleased. [304] \"James McGregor will arrive on the Grangewood tomorrow, Thursday.\" [305] \"What do you plan to do?\" [306] \"We must find him immediately and explain the serious consequences of this. We should ask him to delay the publication of the memoir at least for a month, and we must edit it carefully.\" [307] \"What if he says 'No, sir!' or 'Go to hell!'?\" said the Earl of Carringham. [308] \"I'm afraid that's what he might say.\" Lord Runciman said calmly. \"So, I suddenly thought, inviting him to the Chimney House might be a good idea. He would be delighted to meet the Prince Michael, and it would be much easier to deal with him.\" [309] \"I disagree.\" said the Earl of Carringham hastily. \"I can't get along with Canadians, especially those who have lived in Africa.\" [310] \"He might be a very good person, a promising candidate.\" [311] \"I refuse, Romax. I firmly oppose, find someone else to deal with him.\" [312] \"I have a brilliant idea,\" said Roxane, \"a woman! Find a woman to deal with him, tell him a little, but not too much. Women can cleverly make him understand the situation without angering him. I must admit that I am not in favor of women in politics, but even though Saint Stephen's Cathedral is now a complete ruin, women can still play a magical role in their own domains. Look at Henry's wife, she was a great help to him. Maria is truly remarkable, an exceptional and perfect political wife.\" [313] \"Are you going to invite Masha to the party?\" asked the Marquess of Carrington, looking a little pale at the mention of his formidable sister-in-law. [314] \"No, no, you misunderstood. I was just giving an example of a woman's influence. I still suggest finding a young, attractive, beautiful, and intelligent woman.\" [315] \"Bendel is no good either, she can't help at all, she's just a fanatical socialist. If you tell her that, she'll laugh herself silly.\" [316] \"I haven't considered Miss Elinor. Your daughter is very charming, but she's just a child. We need a clever, calm, and versatile girl. I have a perfect alternative, my cousin Virginia.\" [317] \"Mrs. Rue?\" the Earl of Carringham smiled, starting to think the party might be interesting after all. \"That's a great suggestion, Romax. She's the most attractive woman in London.\" [318] \"She's also well-versed in Hesse-Slav. You should remember, her husband worked in the embassy there. And, like you said, she has a lot of personal charm.\" [319] \"A real beauty,\" the Earl murmured. [320] \"Then it's settled.\" [321] Mr. Roxmax finally released his grip on the Earl of Carringham's collar, allowing the Earl to quickly escape. [322] \"Goodbye! Roxanne! You take care of it, please.\" [323] Carterham got into a taxi, he was really sick of George Romex. His red and swollen face, heavy breathing, and bulging serious blue eyes annoyed him. He sighed thinking about the weekend gathering. It was really annoying, very annoying! Then he thought of Virginia Ruff, and his mood brightened up a bit. [324] \"What a human marvel,\" he muttered to himself, \"what a human marvel.\" [325] Chapter 4: The Enchanting Lady\n[326] George Roxton returned directly to Whitehall [1]. As he entered the luxurious building, a flurry of footsteps echoed. It was here that he handled various state affairs. [327] At this time, Bill Everleigh was diligently filing various documents, but the large sofa chair by the window still retained the residual warmth from someone who had just sat there. [328] Bill Everleigh was a likable young man, appearing to be in his mid-twenties, with a robust build and somewhat clumsy movements. Although his appearance was unattractive, he was not unpleasant to look at. His teeth were white and even, and his brown eyes were sincere. [329] \"Has Richardson's report arrived yet?\" [330] \"No, I'll ask him.\" [331] \"No need. Any messages on the phone?\" [332] \"These matters are mainly handled by Miss Oscar, and Mr. Issacson asked if you could have lunch with him at the Savoy Hotel tomorrow.\" [333] \"Have Miss Oscar check my schedule. If there are no other appointments, have her call and accept.\" [334] \"Yes, sir.\" [335] \"Oh, Everly, also call and check Mrs. Ruff's telephone number at 487 Pound Street for me.\" [336] \"Will do.\" Bill picked up the phone book, pretended to look down the list under the surname column, and then slammed the phone book shut and walked over to the desk. He put his hand on the phone receiver, as if suddenly remembering something, and hesitated for a moment. [337] \"Sir, I just remembered that Mrs. Rue's phone line is out. I called her a little while ago.\" [338] George Romax frowned. [339] \"Frustrating,\" he said. \"Very frustrating.\" He tapped the table lightly, hesitating. [340] \"If there's something important, I can go over there in a taxi right now. She should be home.\" [341] George Romax pondered for a moment, with Bill waiting anxiously by his side. If the answer was affirmative, he would rush out immediately. [342] \"Perhaps this is the best course of action,\" Romax finally said. \"You go in a taxi and ask Mrs. Ruff if she'll be home this afternoon at four o'clock. Tell her I have something very important to discuss with her.\" [343] \"Yes, sir.\" Bill grabbed his hat and quickly left. [344] Ten minutes later, a taxi dropped him off at 487 Pound Street. He rang the doorbell and knocked heavily on the door knocker. A serious-looking servant opened the door, and Bill nodded to her, recognizing her as an acquaintance. [345] \"Good morning, Chivers. Is Mrs. Ruff at home?\" [346] \"She is preparing to leave.\" [347] \"Bill, is that you?\" A voice came down the staircase, \"I knew it was you as soon as I heard the pounding on the door. Come up.\" [348] Bill looked up and saw a smiling face peering down at him, a face that always had a special charm that made anyone who saw it, including Bill, lose their composure. He bounded up the stairs two steps at a time, taking the hand that Virginia extended to him. [349] \"Hi, Virginia!\" [350] \"Hello, Bill!\" [351] Attraction is a mysterious thing: there are so many young women in the world, some even more beautiful than Virginia Ruef, and even if they say \"Hello, Bill\" with the same tone, it won't have any special effect. But when Virginia says those simple words, Bill feels spellbound. [352] Virginia was twenty-seven years old, tall and slender with a perfectly proportioned figure that was like poetry. Her hair was a pure copper, with a golden undertone and a hint of green. Her chin was small and determined, her nose cute, and her eyes blue. When she opened her eyelids, they glowed with a deep blue light, and her eyes sparkled. Her lips were always slightly upturned, giving her a wonderful, indescribable \"Venusian air\" that was full of expression. She exuded an aura of vitality, and wherever she went, she was the center of attention, and no one could ignore her charm. [353] She pulled Bill into the small living room, which was decorated in pale purple, green, and yellow, giving the impression of a field of daffodils hiding in the grass. [354] \"Bill, dear,\" Virginia said. \"I'm sure the State Department misses you. They can't function without you, can they?\" [355] \"I'm here to deliver a message from Mr. Kodes,\" Bill said disrespectfully, referring to his boss by his first name. [356] \"Oh, and if he asks, tell him your phone was broken this morning.\" [357] \"But it wasn't.\" [358] \"I know, but I told him it was.\" [359] \"Why? What's the story behind this diplomatic language?\" [360] Bill gives her a reproachful look. [361] \"Only then could I come and see you.\" [362] \"Oh, darling, you're so sweet!\" [363] \"Chivvers just said you were about to go out.\" [364] \"Yes, I'm going to Sloane Street. They've just brought out a lovely new girdle.\" [365] \"Girdle?\" [366] \"Yes, a garter\u2014belt\u2014a tight band worn around the hips.\" [367] \"What a shame! Talking about underwear with a young man like that. It's not very elegant.\" [368] \"But the hips aren't vulgar. Everyone has hips, but we poor women have to go to great lengths to pretend we don't. The garter belt is made of red rubber and goes up to just above the knee. It's a real pain to walk in.\" [369] \"That's terrible!\" said Bill. \"Why would you wear that?\" [370] \"Because women are willing to suffer for their figure. Let's not talk about garter belts anymore. What did George say?\" [371] \"He asked if you'll be home at 4 pm today.\" [372] \"No, I'm going to Larnaca. Why would he want to make such a formal visit? Is he going to propose to me?\" [373] \"If he does, I wouldn't be surprised.\" [374] \"If that's the case, tell him I prefer it when men propose out of the blue, spontaneously.\" [375] \"Like me?\" [376] \"You're not spontaneous, you're habitual.\" [377] \"Virginia, will you\u2026?\" [378] \"Alright, alright, Bill, don't be silly in the morning. You should treat me like a middle-aged woman with maternal instincts. I'll remember your feelings. \" [379] \"Virginia, I really love you.\" [380] \"I know, Bill, I know. I just like feeling loved. Am I bad for that? I hope all the good men in the world like me.\" [381] \"There are enough people who love you already.\" Bill said dejectedly. [382] \"But I don't want George to like me. I don't think he will either, he's married to his career. What else did he say?\" [383] \"He just said he had something important to discuss with you.\" [384] \"I'm getting more and more interested. There aren't many things that make George feel important. I'm not going to Rainerch today, I can go anytime. Tell George I'll be waiting for him at 4 o'clock.\" [385] Bill checked his wristwatch. [386] \"It's a pity to go back before lunch. Let's go out for some food, Virginia.\" [387] \"I'm going out for lunch.\" [388] \"It's okay, just stay with me today. Cancel all other arrangements.\" [389] \"Okay,\" Virginia smiled at him. [390] \"Virginia, you're so nice. You actually like me, don't you? You like me more than others, don't you?\" [391] \"Bill, I really like you. If I had to marry someone, like in a book where a bad guy says to me, 'You have to marry someone, or I'll torture you little by little,' then I'd definitely choose you. I mean it. I'd say, 'Give me Bill!'\" [392] \"So\u2026\"\n[393] \"But I don't have to marry someone. I enjoy being a bad widow.\" [394] \"Married to me, you can still do whatever you want, go anywhere, do anything, without worrying about me.\" [395] \"Bill, you don't understand me. I'm the kind of person who would be passionate if I got married.\" [396] Bill sighed heavily. [397] \"I feel like I'm going to commit suicide someday.\" He grumbled dejectedly. [398] \"You won't. You'll go out to dinner with a pretty girl, like you did the other night.\" [399] Everly was a bit taken aback. [400] \"You mean Dorothy Kirkpatrick, the girl at the 'Hook and Button Restaurant'? Yeah, she's a nice girl, proper, and there's nothing wrong with her.\" [401] \"Of course not. I'm happy when you're happy, but don't pretend to be heartbroken.\" [402] Eversley regained some of his self-esteem. [403] \"Virginia, you don't understand,\" he said sternly, \"men... \"\n[404] \"They all like polygamy! I understand. Sometimes I wonder if I'm in favor of polyandry. If you really love me, take me to lunch right now.\" [405] ***\n[406] Whitehall is a street in the City of London that connects the House of Commons and Downing Street. Along this street and nearby are located the Ministry of Defence, the Foreign and Commonwealth Office, the Home Office, and the Admiralty, among other British government departments. Therefore, \"Whitehall\" is often used as a synonym for the British civil service. [407] Chapter 5: The First Night in London\n[408] Even the most meticulous plans have flaws. George Romilly made a mistake that created a fatal flaw in his plan, and Bill was that flaw. [409] Bill was a good kid who excelled in cricket and golf. He was elegant in his manner and amiable in his disposition. He got his job at the Foreign Office through his charm rather than his intellect, and he was well-suited for the position. His job required little responsibility and no brainpower, and he was like a little dog at George's side. His task was to be at George's beck and call, to run errands, and to make himself useful in any way he could. Bill did all of this diligently. When George was away, Bill would flop into the largest sofa chair and comfortably read the sports news, a tradition that had been going on for a long time. [410] George had grown accustomed to sending Bill on errands, and this time he sent Bill to the office of the Whitehall Shipping Company to inquire about the arrival time of the Granaanburgh. Like many well-bred British young men, Bill spoke with a pleasant voice but with some difficulty in pronunciation. The words \"Granaanburgh\" that he uttered would have made any speechmaster frown and sound awkward and out of place. The clerk at the shipping company heard \"Carnfrae.\" [411] The clerk said that Carnfrae would arrive on the following Thursday, and Bill thanked him before leaving. George Romilly received the message and adjusted his schedule accordingly. He had no knowledge of the Union Castle Line's sailing schedule and assumed that Jimmy McGlashan would arrive on Thursday. [412] Therefore, on Tuesday morning of that week, when he kept the Earl of Carringham waiting on the club steps, he was still unaware that the Granada had docked at Southampton the previous afternoon. At 2 pm that afternoon, Anthony Kede, a traveler with the name Jimmy Romilly, stepped out of the cabin and hailed a taxi at Waterloo Station. After a moment's thought, he instructed the driver to head to the Britz Hotel. [413] \"It's all right to relax now,\" Anthony said, gazing out the window with interest. [414] It had been fourteen years since he had last left London. [415] After checking into the hotel, he went for a walk along the riverbank. It was a joyous feeling to be back in London, although everything had changed. The Blackfriars Bridge area used to be a small restaurant where he and his friends often dined. He was a socialist back then, always wearing a red scarf. He was so young at the time. [416] He turned around and began to retrace his steps back to the hotel. Just as he was crossing the street, a man bumped into him full force, almost causing him to fall. Both men steadied themselves, and the man, who was of short stature with a muscular build, began to mutter an apology while staring intently at Anthony's face. He had the appearance of a typical working-class man, and seemed to be a foreigner. [417] Anthony was pondering the man's inquisitive gaze as he walked back. It was rather peculiar! Perhaps it was the man's tanned complexion that stood out among the pale Londoners. He returned to his room and, on a whim, walked up to the mirror to examine himself closely.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was expelled from Oxford University for misbehavior. Some say he died in Congo two years later, but it's just a rumor. He reappeared a few months ago when there were rumors of the monarchists plotting a comeback.\" [1408] \"Really?\" said the chief of police. \"Where did he appear?\" [1409] \"In America.\" [1410] \"America!\" [1411] Balthazar turned to Isakovsky and asked tersely, \"Oil?\" [1412] The big financier nodded. [1413] \"What he means is that, as a candidate for the throne of Hesse-Slav, he would be more popular than Prince Michael, because of his modern and enlightened ideas, and his early support for democratic and republican ideals. If the American consortium could support him, he would grant them a concession in return.\" [1414] At this point, Balthazar had forgotten his habit of keeping his emotions in check and whistled a long, low whistle. [1415] \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"On the other hand, the monarchists support Prince Michael. But you think you have the upper hand. And then this happened.\" [1416] \"You don't think\u2026\" George said. [1417] \"It's a big deal,\" the sheriff said. \"That's what Mr. Isakov said. A big deal, must be a very big deal.\" [1418] \"There will always be underhanded situations,\" Isakov said calmly. \"Now, Wall Street has the upper hand. But we haven't settled our differences yet. Sheriff, if you want to serve your country, find the killer of Prince Michael.\" [1419] \"There's one thing that strikes me as suspicious,\" George interjected. \"Why didn't Captain Androidsi come with the prince yesterday?\" [1420] \"I've already looked into it,\" Balthazar said. \"It's quite simple. He stayed in the city to meet a young lady for Prince Michael to arrange a date for the following weekend. The baron disapproves of this, believing it to be unwise at this stage. So the prince had to do it secretly. He, you could say, is quite a free-spirited young man.\" [1421] \"Probably,\" said George stiffly. \"Ah, probably.\" [1422] \"There's one more thing we need to keep in mind,\" Bartel hesitated. \"Victor King should be in Britain right now.\" [1423] \"Victor King?\" Ramecs furrowed his brow, trying to recall the name. [1424] \"That infamous French con artist, sir. The French security agency has warned us.\" [1425] \"Oh,\" said Ramecs. \"I remember now. The jewel thief, right? What? The\u2026\"\n[1426] Bartlett quickly gave him a meaningful look, and he cut off the rest of his sentence. Eisakistan sat by the fireplace, absent-mindedly frowning. He looked up, sensing the change in the atmosphere, feeling the tension that had settled in the air. [1427] \"Roxmeyer, is there anything else you need?\" he asked. [1428] \"No, thank you, old friend.\" [1429] \"Inspector Bartlett, will it cause any trouble if I go back to London now?\" [1430] \"I'm afraid so, sir,\" the inspector said politely, \"If you left, others would have to leave too, and that would be unacceptable.\" [1431] \"Very well,\" the great financier left the room, closing the door behind him. [1432] \"Isaak is quite remarkable,\" George muttered carelessly. [1433] \"He's a very imposing person,\" Inspector Battles agreed. [1434] George began pacing back and forth again. [1435] \"What you said just now really annoyed me,\" he said. \"I thought Victor was in prison!\" [1436] \"He got out a few months ago. The French police planned to keep an eye on him, but he slipped away. It's child's play for him, he's the coolest guy in the world. For various reasons, they believe he's in Britain now, so they informed us.\" [1437] \"But what's he doing in Britain?\" [1438] \"That's for you to tell me, sir.\" Batters said meaningfully. [1439] \"What do you mean? You think... Of course, you know the ins and outs of the matter. I know you do. I hadn't started my job yet, and the whole thing was told to me by the late Earl of Carterham. It was a disaster unprecedented!\" [1440] \"The Diamond of Light Mountain.\" Chief Batters pondered. [1441] \"Shh!\" George alertly looked around, \"Please, don't mention the name, or better yet, use K as a substitute when you have to.\" [1442] Bartholomew returned to his expressionless demeanor. [1443] \"You don't think Victor King is involved in this murder, do you, Bartholomew?\" [1444] \"It's possible. Think about it, there were only four places where the royal visitor could have hidden the diamond, and the Chimney House was one of them. That's where K disappeared three days later, and Victor King was arrested in Paris. Everyone has been hoping that someday, we could find the diamond through him.\" [1445] \"But the Chimney House has been turned upside down many times.\" [1446] \"Yes.\" Batter spoke sharply, \"But if you don't know where to look, just searching aimlessly is useless. It's just a hypothesis now, that Victor King came here to find that thing and accidentally ran into Michael Prince. Then he killed him.\" [1447] \"That's possible.\" George said, \"That could very well be the truth of the case.\" [1448] \"I don't think it's that high a probability, just a certain possibility.\" [1449] \"Why?\" [1450] \"Victor King has never been known to kill anyone.\" Batter said solemnly. [1451] \"Oh, but he's a dangerous criminal...\"\n[1452] Batter shook his head discontentedly: \"Mr. Romax, the behavior of criminals has their own patterns, which is quite surprising. But...\"\n[1453] \"What?\" [1454] \"It's better to ask the servants of Prince Michael. I specifically left him until last. If you don't mind, please call him here.\" [1455] George agreed, and the detective rang the bell. Treadwell was summoned and left after receiving the instructions. [1456] After a while, he returned with a person. The person was tall, with blond hair, high cheekbones, and deep blue eyes. His face was expressionless, almost matching Battert's. [1457] \"Are you Boris Anchukov?\" [1458] \"Yes.\" [1459] \"Are you the personal servant of Michael Prince?\" [1460] \"Yes.\" He spoke English well, but with a distinct foreign accent. [1461] \"Do you know that your master was murdered last night?\" [1462] The man did not answer, but only let out a wild animal-like howl. George jumped back, hastily retreating towards the window. [1463] \"Where was the last time you saw your master?\" [1464] \"The prince retired at ten o'clock. As usual, I slept in the small room next to his. He must have gone through another door to the rooms downstairs, the one on the corridor. I didn't hear him go out. Maybe I was drugged. I'm a negligent servant. My master is awake, and I'm sleeping. I should be damned.\" [1465] George stared at him intently, somewhat dazed. [1466] \"You have a lot of affection for your master?\" Batter asked, keeping a close eye on the other's movements. [1467] Baxter's face contorted with pain. He struggled to speak twice before his voice became shrill with emotion. [1468] \"British police, I tell you, I would die for him. Now he's dead, and I'm still alive, and if I don't avenge him, I'll die with my eyes closed and never find peace. I'll hunt down the murderer like a hound, and once I find him... Ah!\" He spoke with fire in his eyes. [1469] Suddenly, he pulled a large knife from his coat and waved it in the air. \"I won't kill him right away. No, I'll cut off his nose first, then chop off his ears, and gouge out his eyes. Then, I'll stab him in the heart with this knife!\" [1470] He turned and quickly put the knife back in its place, leaving the room. George stared intently at the door, which was now closed, and his already protruding eyes seemed ready to burst from their sockets. [1471] \"A true Hessian,\" he muttered. [1472] \"A savage race, a bunch of bandits,\" Balthar the constable alertly stood up. [1473] \"Either he's truly devoted or he's just putting on a show,\" he commented. \"If it's the former, God help the killer when he's caught.\" [1474] Chapter 15: The Stranger from France\n[1475] Virginia and Anthony walked side by side along the path to the lake. After leaving the room, neither of them spoke for a few minutes until Virginia finally broke the silence with a giggle. [1476] \"Hey,\" she said, \"wasn't that scary? I have a lot to tell you and a lot to ask, but I don't know where to start. First, in a low voice, \"how did you handle the body? It sounds terrifying, and I never thought I'd be involved in a crime.\" [1477] \"It's new to you, isn't it?\" Anthony agreed. [1478] \"Isn't it to you?\" [1479] \"Well, I've never handled a body before.\" [1480] \"Tell me about it.\" [1481] He walked across the platform again, retracing his steps from the night before. Anthony briefly recounted the events to Virginia, who listened intently. [1482] \"You're so clever,\" she complimented him when he finished. \"When I get back to Paddington station, I can collect my luggage. The only difficulty is what to say if someone asks where you were last night.\" [1483] \"It won't happen. The body wouldn't have been discovered until late last night or early this morning. Otherwise, it would have been in the newspaper this morning. The things you're thinking of are from detective novels. Forensic scientists aren't that infallible in determining the exact time of death. The time of death is vague, so the alibi for last night is much more important.\" [1484] \"I know, the Duke of Cambridge told me. But the police believe you're innocent, don't they?\" [1485] Anthony didn't answer right away. [1486] \"He doesn't seem like a particularly alert person,\" Virginia continued. [1487] \"I don't know,\" Anthony answered slowly. \"I think Inspector Bates is very astute. He seems to believe I'm innocent, but I'm not entirely sure. He mainly thinks I don't have a clear motive for murder.\" [1488] \"Clear motive?\" Virginia exclaimed. \"But why would you want to kill a complete stranger?\" [1489] Anthony gave her a sharp look. [1490] \"Have you ever been to Herschovitz?\" he asked. [1491] \"Yes, I followed my husband to the British embassy there for two years.\" [1492] \"Just so happens to be the two years before the king and queen were assassinated. Have you met Prince Michael O'Paulus?\" [1493] \"Michael? Of course. A poor thing! He even asked me to marry him, despite our differences in status.\" [1494] \"Really? What about your husband?\" [1495] \"He said we could follow the story of David and Uriah [1].\" [1496] \"How did you respond to his advances?\" [1497] \"Well,\" said Virginia, \"one must use diplomacy in all situations. I didn't outright reject poor Michael, but he was still hurt and gave up. Why are you so interested in Michael?\" [1498] \"Just trying to learn more, if you don't mind. You didn't see the victim, did you?\" [1499] \"No, he arrived and went straight to his room to rest.\" [1500] \"So you didn't see the body?\" [1501] Virginia looked at him curiously and shook her head. [1502] \"Do you think you could possibly take a look?\" [1503] \"With the influence of a high-ranking person, such as the Earl of Carrington, I think it should be possible. Why? Is this an order?\" [1504] \"My goodness, no,\" Anthony said, surprised. \"Would I be so presumptuous? It's just that Count Stanislaus could be Prince Michael of Hesse-Darmstadt.\" [1505] Virginia's eyes widened. [1506] \"I see,\" her face suddenly lit up with a charming smile. \"You don't think Michael hid in the room because he didn't want to see me, do you?\" [1507] \"Something like that,\" Anthony admitted. \"Someone wanted to stop you from coming to the Smoke House, and if I'm not mistaken, it's because you know about Hesse-Lobenstein.\" [1508] \"You mean the victim was an imposter?\" Virginia exclaimed suddenly. [1509] \"It's just a possibility I thought of. If you can get the Earl of Carringham to take you to see the body, we'll be able to figure it out right away.\" [1510] \"The time of death was 11:45,\" Virginia mused. \"Just when the note mentioned, it's all too extraordinary.\" [1511] \"You mean that window over there?\" Virginia asked, pointing to the window above the meeting room. \"The second one from that side?\" [1512] \"No, my room is on the other side, over by Elizabeth's side hall. What's going on?\" [1513] \"Last night, I thought I heard gunshots and went back, and the lights in that room turned on.\" [1514] \"That's strange! I don't know who's staying there, but I can ask Bunder. They might have heard the gunshots too.\" [1515] \"If so, they haven't said anything about it today. I heard from Inspector Bartlett that no one in the house heard gunshots. That's my only lead, not very reliable, but I'm still going to pursue it.\" [1516] \"It's really strange,\" Virginia pondered. [1517] They walked to the boathouse by the lake, leaning against the wall and talking. [1518] \"Let's go over everything now,\" Anthony said, \"let's take our time and row out on the lake so that the police, the American guests, and the gossiping maid can't overhear us.\" [1519] \"I heard some things from the Earl of Carringham,\" Virginia said, \"but not much. Tell me, are you Anthony Kidd or Jimmy McGregor?\" [1520] So, Anthony recounted the story he had told earlier, but with a slight difference in what he said to Virginia. From six weeks ago until he unexpectedly recognized the \"Mr. Holmes\" figure. [1521] \"By the way, Mrs. Rue,\" he said finally. \"I haven't thanked you yet for lying about me being your old friend.\" [1522] \"You are my old friend, aren't you?\" Virginia exclaimed. \"You don't think I'd have pushed the body on you and then pretend we're just acquaintances when we meet again, do you? I'm not that kind of person.\" She paused and then continued, \"You know, there's one thing that really surprises me. I had no idea that the memoir had such an unexpected mystery.\" [1523] \"You're right,\" Anthony agreed. \"There's one thing I want to ask you.\" [1524] \"What is it?\" [1525] \"When I mentioned Jimmy McGregor's name to you yesterday on Pound Street, why did you seem so surprised? Had you heard of him before?\" [1526] \"Sherlock Holmes, I have heard of him. George, my cousin George Roxborough, came to me one day and talked about a bunch of nonsense. He wanted me to come here and try to seduce this McGregor into giving up the memoir. Of course, he didn't say it like that. He talked about British ladies and all that useless stuff, but his intentions were clear. It's only poor old George who could come up with such a stupid idea. Later, I asked him a few more questions, and he started to lie to me. His lies were so bad that even a two-year-old child couldn't be fooled.\" [1527] \"Anyway, his plan seems to have succeeded,\" Anthony said. \"I came here, just as he wanted me to be Jimmy McGregor, and you were just what he wanted.\" [1528] \"But there's no memoir for poor old George! Now I have a question for you. When I said those letters weren't written by me, you said you knew. How did you know that?\" [1529] \"I do know,\" Anthony said with a smile. \"My psychological knowledge is quite solid.\" [1530] \"You mean you have confidence in my character, so...\"\n[1531] Anthony shook his head vigorously. [1532] \"Not at all. I know nothing about your character. You might have a lover and write him letters. But you wouldn't give in to blackmail. The real Virginia Ruff was terrified, but you held out.\" [1533] \"I'm curious about who the real 'Virginia Ruff' is, I mean where she is. It makes me feel like I have a doppelganger.\" [1534] Anthony lit a cigarette. [1535] \"Did you know that one of the letters was mailed from the Chimney House?\" he asked. [1536] \"What?\" Virginia was startled, \"When was the letter?\" [1537] \"There was no date on the letter, but it was quite strange.\" [1538] \"I'm sure there's no other Virginia Ruffin who has stayed at the Chimney House besides me, and if there was, the Earl of Carringham or Bunder would have told me about the coincidence of the name.\" [1539] \"Hmm, that's quite eerie. Mrs. Ruffin, I'm starting to deeply doubt the existence of the other Virginia Ruffin.\" [1540] \"She's elusive,\" Virginia agreed. [1541] \"She's hard to pin down. I think the letter-writer may have used your name intentionally.\" [1542] \"But why?\" Virginia exclaimed, \"Why would they do that?\" [1543] \"That's the very question, there's so much to investigate.\" [1544] \"Who do you think killed Michael?\" Virginia asked suddenly, \"The Red Hand Gang?\" [1545] \"Possibly,\" Anthony said, his tone conveying his displeasure, \"They have a tendency to kill innocent people.\" [1546] \"Let's get started then,\" Virginia said, \"I saw the Earl of Carrington and Lady Bander walking. Our first task is to determine if the deceased was indeed Michael.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[151] \"Your comments about the Middle Ages don't seem to interest you. Let's talk about the Earl of Stapleton instead. It's quite foolish for a diplomat to send something from France to Africa and then to England. If he really wanted you to have the thousand pounds, he should have put it in his will. Fortunately, neither of us is so proud as to refuse a bequest. Stapleton is really crazy.\" [152] \"Do you really think so?\" [153] Anthony furrowed his brow and continued pacing around the room. [154] \"Have you seen that thing?\" he suddenly asked. [155] \"What thing?\" [156] \"That manuscript.\" [157] \"What on earth for? I don't see the point in looking at that thing.\" [158] Anthony smiled. [159] \"I was just wondering. You know, there are often troubles caused by biographies and revelations. It seems that people who are silent for most of their lives like to stir up some trouble when they die, feeling a kind of malicious satisfaction. Jimmy, what kind of person is Lord Steppendale? You've met him, talked to him, and you're good at judging people. Do you think he's like a vengeful old devil?\" [160] Jimmy shook his head. [161] \"It's hard to say. You see, he was drunk the first night; the next day, he turned into a noble old man, polite and flattering to the point of making me feel embarrassed.\" [162] \"Did he say anything special when he was drunk?\" [163] Jimmy furrowed his brow, recalling the situation. [164] \"He said he knows where the Light Mountain Diamond is.\" [165] \"Well...,\" Anthony said, \"everyone knows that the diamond is displayed in the Tower of London's exhibition room, under a thick glass case, guarded by iron bars, and surrounded by well-dressed people.\" [166] \"That's right.\" Jimmy agreed. [167] \"Did Stapleton mention anything else like that? For example, he knew where Wallace was hiding it in a city.\" [168] Jimmy shook his head. [169] \"Uh...,\" Anthony replied, then lit a cigarette and paced back and forth. [170] \"You probably never read the papers, you uncultured swine.\" After a moment, he asked. [171] \"Not too often,\" McGregor answered simply. \"There's not much in the paper that interests me.\" [172] \"Fortunately, I'm more civilized than you. There have been frequent mentions of Heslovich in the papers lately, which could be a sign that the monarchy is making a comeback.\" [173] \"But Nicholas IV has no heirs,\" Jimmy said. \"But I always feel that the House of Opolovitch is not yet extinct, with young blood flowing in, like cousins, distant cousins, and even more distant cousins.\" [174] \"So, finding a heir to the throne is not difficult?\" [175] \"Not at all,\" Jimmy answered. \"If they ever get tired of the republic, I wouldn't be surprised. Those tough guys are used to assassinating kings, and shooting a president just doesn't have the same thrill or challenge. As for kings, I just remembered something Steptoe said that night, that he knew the thugs who attacked him, they were from Viktor's court.\" [176] \"What?\" Anthony whirled around. [177] McGrath's mouth curved into a wider smile. [178] \"Getting excited, Mr. Gents?\" he drawled. [179] \"Be serious, Jimmy. This is quite important.\" [180] He walked over to the window and stood there, gazing out. [181] \"Who's Victor King?\" Jimmy asked. \"Another royal family from a Balkan country?\" [182] \"No,\" Anthony said slowly. \"He's not that kind of king.\" [183] \"Then who is he?\" [184] Anthony paused for a moment before saying, \"Jimmy, he's a con artist, a notorious jewel thief, and a brazen one at that. Victor King is just a codename he used in Paris. Paris is his gang's headquarters, and that's where he was caught. But they couldn't find enough evidence for his heavy crimes, so they gave him a light sentence of seven years. He's probably out by now, or maybe already has been.\" [185] \"Do you think Count Stapleton's imprisonment has anything to do with him? Were those thugs following him for revenge?\" [186] \"I don't know,\" Anthony said. \"It doesn't seem like it on the surface. As far as I know, Victor King hasn't touched the Hessian royal family. But it all looks so suspicious, doesn't it? Count Stapleton's death, that memoir, the rumors in the papers \u2013 everything is vague and intriguing. And I've also heard that Hessia has discovered oil. James, I have a strong feeling that they've been eyeing that insignificant little country.\" [187] \"Who?\" [188] \"The Hebrews, the yellow-faced financial moguls sitting in their offices.\" [189] \"So, what's your point?\" [190] \"Just making things complicated, that's all.\" [191] \"Not that,\" Anthony said, slightly disappointed. \"Guess where I'm planning to go with those two hundred and fifty pounds?\" [192] \"South America?\" [193] \"No, I'm going to Hesslovia. I think I could stand with that republic, and maybe even become its president.\" [194] \"Why don't you just claim to be a descendant of the Paulovich bloodline and go there to be a king?\" [195] \"No, James. A king has to reign for life, while a president only serves for four years. For me, a country like Hesslovia, four years is just right.\" [196] \"A king might not even last four years,\" Jimmy interjected. [197] \"I might not be able to resist taking a thousand pounds from your share. By the time you've brought back the gold, a thousand pounds won't even be a blip on your radar. So, I'll invest it in Hersovlav's oil mines. You know, James? This idea just keeps getting better and better. I never would have thought of Hersovlav if you hadn't mentioned it. I'll spend a day in London, grab the thousand pounds, and then catch the Balkan Express.\" [198] \"Don't rush off so quickly. I actually have something else I want you to do for me.\" [199] Anthony sat down on the chair, looking serious. [200] \"I always felt like there was something else, and sure enough, it's all coming together.\" [201] \"It's nothing like that. I just want you to do a small favor for a lady.\" [202] \"Not a chance. I'm not getting involved in your love affairs.\" [203] \"It's not like that. I haven't even met the woman. Let me tell you the whole story.\" [204] \"If you're going to ramble on, I'll have to have another drink.\" [205] The host hastened to oblige, and then began to relate the tale. [206] \"It happened in Uganda. I saved a South European man\u2026\"\n[207] \"James, if I were you, I'd write a book called 'The People I've Saved.' This is the second story I've heard tonight.\" [208] \"This time, I didn't do much. I just pulled him out of the river. It seems that most South Europeans can't swim.\" [209] \"Wait, is this related to something else?\" [210] \"No, not really. But I just remembered something strange. Although we call him Pedro, which is a Dutch name, he's actually from Hesse.\" [211] Anthony nodded indifferently, \"It doesn't matter what you call him, they're all South Europeans. Go on.\" [212] \"The guy might have been grateful, so he followed me like a dog. About six months later, he died of a fever. Before he passed away, he called me over and whispered a secret in my ear using some jargon, I thought he was talking about a gold mine, then he shoved a small oilcloth package into my hand. Well, I didn't take it seriously at the time. It wasn't until a week later that I opened the package. I admit, I was just too curious. I knew that with Pedro's intelligence, even if he saw a gold mine, he might not recognize it, but what if it was really a gold mine?\" [213] \"The thought of gold must have made your heart race, it's hard to resist,\" Anthony interrupted him. [214] \"I've never felt so disgusted in my life. Gold mine? Ha. For that scumbag, that thing could have been a gold mine. Do you know what it was? A letter from a woman, yes, a British woman. The bitch was blackmailing another woman, and she had the nerve to give me that filthy thing.\" [215] \"Your integrity is admirable, James, but South Europeans are South Europeans. His intentions were good. You saved his life, so he left his livelihood for you.\" [216] \"What am I supposed to do with this thing? Burn it? That's what I thought at first, but then I thought about that poor woman who wouldn't know the letters were destroyed and would be living in constant fear of that South European showing up again.\" [217] \"I never thought you had such an imaginative mind.\" Anthony said, lighting a cigarette, \"You're right, that makes it more complicated. Should we mail it to her?\" [218] \"Like most women, she didn't leave dates or addresses on most of the letters, but there was one with what looked like an address information, but only a name: Chimney Cottage.\" [219] Anthony was about to blow out the match in his hand when he suddenly froze, only to flick it away when the flame burned his fingers. [220] \"Chimney Cottage?\" he said. \"That's too bizarre.\" [221] \"Do you know it?\" [222] \"Darling, it's a British mansion, where the king and queen often spend weekends, and diplomats gather there.\" [223] \"I'd rather you go to England than me, because you know all about these things. I'm just a Canadian country bumpkin who would make a mess of things. You're worldly, you've been to Eton and Harrow\u2026\"\n[224] \"Only Eton.\" Anthony said modestly. [225] \"Someone like you could handle this. You were asking me why I didn't just mail them to her? I thought it was too dangerous. I suspect her husband is jealous. What if he accidentally opened them? Or maybe the woman is dead, because the letters seem quite old. I think the only proper thing to do is to take those letters to England and hand them to her personally.\" [226] Anthony dropped his cigarette and walked over to his friend, patting him affectionately on the back. [227] \"You're a real knight in shining armor, Jimmy,\" he said. \"Your little Canadian town should be proud of you. If I were in your shoes, I wouldn't do half as well.\" [228] \"So you're agreeing?\" [229] \"Of course.\" [230] McGregor stood up, walked over to the drawer, took out a packet of letters, and dropped them on the table. [231] \"Here, you better take a look at these first.\" [232] \"Do I really need to?\" [233] \"Based on what you told me about the chimney house, she probably just stayed there for a while. We should go through these letters and see if we can find any clues to figure out where she really lives.\" [234] \"I agree with you.\" [235] They carefully went through all the letters, but found nothing. Anthony thoughtfully gathered the letters together. [236] \"Poor thing,\" he said, \"she must have been terrified.\" [237] Jimmy nodded. [238] \"Do you think you can find her?\" he asked, worriedly. [239] \"I'll stay in Britain if I can't find her. You seem to care a lot about this stranger, James.\" [240] Jimmy absent-mindedly stroked the signature on the letter. [241] \"Her name is lovely,\" he explained. \"Her name is Virginia Ruff.\" [242] * * *\n[243] [1] A ducat, a currency used in Europe during the Middle Ages. [244] Chapter Three: The Troubles of the Upper Class\n[245] \"Indeed, my friend, indeed,\" the Earl of Carrington said, dismissively. [246] The Earl of Carrington, Edward Alastair Brent, was the ninth Earl of Carrington. He was short and shabbily dressed, looking nothing like a nobleman. His eyes were pale blue, and his long, narrow nose gave him a somewhat melancholy appearance. His manners, while somewhat stiff, were impeccable. [247] For the ninth Earl of Carrington, the greatest misfortune of his life was inheriting his brother's title four years ago. The previous Earl had been a household name in England, a prominent figure in the government, and a key player in the British Parliament. His country estate, the Smokestack Villa, was renowned for its hospitality. His wife, the daughter of the Duke of Posh, had played a big part in that. There were always informal gatherings at the Smokestack Villa on weekends, where glasses were raised and deals were made and broken. The who's who of British and European aristocracy had all spent time there. [248] All of that was well and good, but the ninth Earl of Carrington couldn't help but feel a sense of awe when he thought of his brother, Henry, who had done all of that. The ninth Earl simply disagreed with the idea of Smokestack Villa being considered a national asset rather than a private country house. He hated it when non-politicians talked politics, which is why George Romax's incessant prattle was so irritating. Romax was a robust man, slightly overweight, with a ruddy complexion and bulging eyes that exuded an air of self-importance. [249] \"Do you understand what I mean, Carrington? We simply cannot afford any scandal at this point. The situation is delicate,\" Romax said. [250] \"You understand my meaning, Carrington? We cannot afford any scandal at this point. The situation is delicate.\" [251] \"Always has been.\" Lord Carrington replied with a touch of sarcasm. [252] \"I'm a man of action, Carrington.\" [253] \"Indeed, indeed.\" Lord Carrington reverted to his previous tactic. [254] \"If there is any mistake in Hesslovakia, we are done for. The most important thing is to grant the oil drilling rights to a British company. Please pay attention, will you?\" [255] \"Of course, of course.\" [256] \"Prince Michael O'Paulwich arrives this weekend, organizing a hunting party at the Chimney House to provide a cover, and the whole thing will be wrapped up.\" [257] \"I was planning to go abroad this week, actually,\" said the Earl of Carringham. [258] \"Nonsense, my dear Carringham! Who goes abroad in the first week of October?\" [259] \"My doctor thinks my health is very poor,\" said the Earl of Carringham, gazing longingly at the taxi passing by. [260] However, he could not escape, as Roxemus always clung to anyone he spoke to about serious matters, and this annoying habit of his had been going on for some time. In this particular situation, he was holding tightly onto the Earl of Carringham's collar. [261] \"Brother, I solemnly entrust this to you. It's a time of national crisis, and what's coming\u2026\"\n[262] The Earl of Carrington fidgeted uncomfortably. He suddenly felt that he would rather organize a hundred more parties than listen to Lord Roxane rambling on with his own speech for twenty more minutes. Based on experience, he knew that Lord Roxane would continue to speak for at least another twenty minutes. [263] \"All right,\" he said quickly, \"I'll take care of it, so you arrange everything.\" [264] \"There's nothing to arrange. The Chimney House is the perfect place, and it has so much history. I'll go to the convent, which is only about seven miles away. I won't attend the banquet, as it's not appropriate.\" [265] \"Yes, it's not appropriate,\" agreed the Earl of Carrington, although he didn't know why it wasn't appropriate, and he didn't care to find out. [266] \"However, we should invite Bill Eversley. He can help pass on the message.\" [267] \"That would be splendid,\" the Earl of Carringham perked up a bit, \"Bill is a good shot, and Bunder likes him.\" [268] \"Shooting isn't important; it's just a cover.\" [269] The Earl of Carringham's spirits sank again, and he looked dejected. [270] \"That's all right. The King, the King's retinue, Bill Eversley, Herman Eisencastle...\n[271] \"Who?\" [272] \"Herman Eisencamp. The representative from the consortium I mentioned.\" [273] \"The British consortium?\" [274] \"Yes, what about it?\" [275] \"Nothing, nothing. I was just curious. The name is quite unusual.\" [276] \"By the way, we still need one or two outsiders to make it look legit. Miss Eileen will certainly notice that. We need to find young, non-gossipy, and politically clueless ones.\" [277] \"Bonder will notice that.\" [278] \"I just thought of something.\" Roxas suddenly looked like he remembered something, \"Do you remember what I said earlier?\" [279] \"You said a lot of things.\" [280] \"I mean the unfortunate incident.\" He lowered his voice and whispered into the ear of the Marquis, \"Staunton's memoirs.\" [281] \"I think you're overreacting.\" Sir Henry restrained himself from yawning, \"People like scandals. I myself also read some memoirs and found them very interesting.\" [282] \"It's not whether people read or don't read, they will all rush to read it, and that's the problem. At this critical moment, publishing such a book will ruin everything, everything! The people of Hesse-Slavonia hope for the restoration. Prince Michael has the support and encouragement of His Majesty the King, and the people of Hesse-Slavonia are also prepared to acclaim him.\" [283] \"Who is the one who is preparing to grant the oil concession to the Herman Eisakstein Company to obtain a million loans for succession?\" [284] \"Carterham, Carterham,\" Roamax begged in a painful tone in his ear, \"be careful, you must be careful.\" [285] \"So the question is... \" The Marquis lowered his voice and continued with interest, \"The events mentioned in Staunton's memoirs will make their dreams come true, mainly the atrocities and vices of the House of Paulovich, right? Then the question will be raised in the parliament: why change the current liberal democracy to a backward autocratic rule? Just because of the policies of the bloodsucking capitalists? Then there will be calls for 'down with the government'... Is that right?\" [286] Roxane nodded.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."